1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:00,765
Good morning,

2
00:00:00,765 --> 00:00:06,700
and welcome to Oniton Hall, one of the largest estates in the area.

3
00:00:06,700 --> 00:00:08,563
My name’s Nick,

4
00:00:08,563 --> 00:00:09,960
and I’m one of the guides.

5
00:00:09,960 --> 00:00:15,524
I’ll give you a brief introduction to the estate while you’re sitting

6
00:00:15,524 --> 00:00:15,765
down,

7
00:00:15,765 --> 00:00:16,850
and then we’ll walk round.

8
00:00:16,850 --> 00:00:23,670
The estate consists of the house, gardens, parkland and farm,

9
00:00:23,670 --> 00:00:27,020
and it dates back to the fourteenth century.

10
00:00:27,020 --> 00:00:32,183
The original house was replaced in the late seventeenth century,

11
00:00:32,183 --> 00:00:35,880
and of course it has had a large number of owners.

12
00:00:35,880 --> 00:00:43,746
Almost all of them have left their mark, generally by adding new rooms, like the ballroom

13
00:00:43,746 --> 00:00:45,291
and conservatory,

14
00:00:45,291 --> 00:00:48,040
or by demolishing others.

15
00:00:48,040 --> 00:00:55,088
The farm looks much as it’s always done, although the current owner has done

16
00:00:55,088 --> 00:00:58,080
a great deal of work to the flower beds.

17
00:00:58,080 --> 00:01:04,269
In the seventeenth century, the estate was owned by a very wealthy man called

18
00:01:04,269 --> 00:01:06,540
Sir Edward Downes.

19
00:01:06,540 --> 00:01:13,117
His intention was to escape from the world of politics, after years as an

20
00:01:13,117 --> 00:01:14,520
active politician,

21
00:01:14,520 --> 00:01:20,916
and to build a new house worthy of his big collection of books, paintings

22
00:01:20,916 --> 00:01:22,320
and sculptures.

23
00:01:22,320 --> 00:01:26,856
He broke off contact with his former political allies,

24
00:01:26,856 --> 00:01:33,580
and hosted meetings of creative and literary people, like painters and poets.

25
00:01:33,580 --> 00:01:40,448
Unusually for his time, he didn’t care whether his guests were rich

26
00:01:40,448 --> 00:01:43,520
or poor, as long as they had talent.

27
00:01:43,520 --> 00:01:51,638
Big houses like Oniton had dozens of servants until the 1920s or 30s,

28
00:01:51,638 --> 00:01:57,050
and we’ve tried to show what their working lives were like.

29
00:01:57,050 --> 00:02:01,619
Photographs of course don’t give much of an idea,

30
00:02:01,619 --> 00:02:10,215
so instead, as you go round the house, you’ll see volunteers dressed up as

31
00:02:10,215 --> 00:02:12,760
nineteenth-century servants, going about their work.

32
00:02:12,760 --> 00:02:16,534
They’ll explain what they’re doing,

33
00:02:16,534 --> 00:02:18,822
and tell you their recipes,

34
00:02:18,822 --> 00:02:21,130
or what tools they’re using.

35
00:02:21,130 --> 00:02:28,490
We’ve just introduced this feature to replace the audio guide we used to have

36
00:02:28,490 --> 00:02:28,540
available.

37
00:02:28,540 --> 00:02:32,970
I see there are a number of children here with you today.

38
00:02:32,970 --> 00:02:40,487
Well, we have several activities specially for children, like dressing up in the sorts

39
00:02:40,487 --> 00:02:43,333
of clothes that children wore in the past,

40
00:02:43,333 --> 00:02:49,146
and as it’s a fine day, some of you will probably want to play

41
00:02:49,146 --> 00:02:50,910
in the adventure playground.

42
00:02:50,910 --> 00:02:56,065
Our latest addition is child-sized tractors,

43
00:02:56,065 --> 00:03:01,000
that you can drive around the grounds.

44
00:03:01,000 --> 00:03:07,870
We’ll also be going into the farm that’s part of the estate, where there’s

45
00:03:07,870 --> 00:03:07,920
plenty to do.

46
00:03:07,920 --> 00:03:12,126
Most of the buildings date from the eighteenth century,

47
00:03:12,126 --> 00:03:18,810
so you can really step back into an agricultural past.

48
00:03:18,810 --> 00:03:26,350
Until recently, the dairy was where milk from the cows was turned into cheese.

49
00:03:26,350 --> 00:03:29,805
It’s now the place to go for lunch,

50
00:03:29,805 --> 00:03:31,613
or afternoon tea,

51
00:03:31,613 --> 00:03:35,310
or just a cup of coffee and a slice of homemade cake.

52
00:03:35,310 --> 00:03:41,166
The big stone building that dominates the farm is the large barn,

53
00:03:41,166 --> 00:03:46,060
and in here is our collection of agricultural tools.

54
00:03:46,060 --> 00:03:52,445
These were used in the past to plough the earth, sow seeds, make gates,

55
00:03:52,445 --> 00:03:53,770
and much more.

56
00:03:53,770 --> 00:04:00,511
There’s a small barn, also made of stone, where you can groom the donkeys

57
00:04:00,511 --> 00:04:03,200
and horses, to keep their coats clean.

58
00:04:03,200 --> 00:04:06,335
They really seem to enjoy having it done,

59
00:04:06,335 --> 00:04:09,270
and children love grooming them.

60
00:04:09,270 --> 00:04:12,837
The horses no longer live in the stables,

61
00:04:12,837 --> 00:04:19,711
which instead is the place to go to buy gifts, books, our own jams

62
00:04:19,711 --> 00:04:20,452
and pickles,

63
00:04:20,452 --> 00:04:24,420
and clothes and blankets made of wool from our sheep.

64
00:04:24,420 --> 00:04:27,069
Outside the shed,

65
00:04:27,069 --> 00:04:33,149
which is the only brick building, you can climb into a horse-drawn carriage for

66
00:04:33,149 --> 00:04:36,660
a lovely, relaxing tour of the park and farm.

67
00:04:36,660 --> 00:04:40,920
The carriages are well over a hundred years old.

68
00:04:40,920 --> 00:04:44,991
And finally, the parkland,

69
00:04:44,991 --> 00:04:49,142
which was laid out in the eighteenth century, with a lake

70
00:04:49,142 --> 00:04:52,130
and trees that are now well established.

71
00:04:52,130 --> 00:04:57,748
You’ll see types of cattle and sheep that are hardly ever found on farms

72
00:04:57,748 --> 00:04:58,010
these days.

73
00:04:58,010 --> 00:05:03,230
We’re helping to preserve them, to stop their numbers falling further.

74
00:05:03,230 --> 00:05:09,000
OK, well if you’d like to come with me …
